<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <base href="/" />
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    @* No static <title> here: every page supplies its own through <PageTitle>, and with
       prerendering the HeadOutlet emits a real <title> element into this head. A second,
       earlier one would win in the served HTML (browsers and crawlers take the first). *@
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="description" content="Bit.Bmotion demos - a Blazor-native animation library inspired by Framer Motion: springs, gestures, variants, drag, layout and scroll animations." />
    <link rel="icon" href="favicon.ico" type="image/x-icon" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="512x512" href="icon-512.png" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/theme.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/app.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="css/motion-samples.css" />
    @* The theme is applied here, in the head, rather than from js/demo.js at the end of the body:
       the stylesheets key off the data-theme attribute, and setting it after the first paint would
       show the reader a flash of the other theme. This is the same decision js/demo.js makes for
       the app's lifetime - see window.bmTheme there - kept short enough to inline. *@
    <script>
        (function () {
            var stored = null;
            try { stored = localStorage.getItem('bm-theme'); } catch (e) { /* no stored preference */ }
            var theme = (stored === 'light' || stored === 'dark')
                ? stored
                : (window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches ? 'dark' : 'light');
            document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', theme);
        })();
    </script>
    @* Same render mode as the app itself, so <PageTitle> keeps working after hydration. *@
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
</head>

<body>
    @* Prerendered on the server, then hydrated by the WebAssembly runtime. Bit.Bmotion is inert
       during the prerender pass - it only touches the browser from OnAfterRenderAsync and event
       handlers - so the markup is served with every element at its Initial state and the
       animations start once the runtime takes over. See Layout/MainLayout.razor for the
       RendererInfo-driven hydration hint. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />

    <div id="blazor-error-ui">
        An unhandled error has occurred.
        <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
        <span class="dismiss">🗙</span>
    </div>

    @* Loaded before the Blazor script so the window.bm* helpers exist by the time the app boots. *@
    <script src="js/demo.js"></script>
    <script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"></script>
</body>

</html>
